# Workstation DCV AMI Build Guide

Generated by `fabrica workstation ami build` in `{{ .Region }}`.

`fabrica workstation create` is AMI-first. Stock Ubuntu is **not** a DCV AMI:
create fails closed if the image name looks like Canonical Ubuntu 22.04 server.

## Contract

| Requirement | Value |
|---|---|
| Base OS | Ubuntu 22.04 LTS, x86_64 |
| DCV | `dcv` on PATH; `dcvserver.service` enabled |
| SSM Agent | deb or snap unit enabled (fail closed) |
| Runtime | Fabrica cloud-init configures a virtual session and password only |

Do not bake session passwords. GPU drivers are optional (virtual sessions work
on CPU; artist/g6 templates still want a GPU AMI).

## Generated files

- `component.yaml` — installs NICE DCV (Ubuntu 22.04 tarball) and SSM Agent
- `image-builder-recipe.json` — parent `{{ .BaseImage }}`; root volume `/dev/sda1` encrypted
- `verify-ami.sh` — fail-closed bake gate (read-only): every EBS snapshot of the
  baked AMI must be encrypted and tagged `ManagedBy=fabrica`

## Bake with an encrypted root volume

The AMI's root snapshot must be encrypted. Encrypt the volume the bake runs
on, so the snapshot taken from it is encrypted when it is created. Do not bake
unencrypted and then `copy-image --encrypted`: that leaves an unencrypted AMI
and snapshot behind to clean up, and that AMI could be recorded by mistake.
No `kmsKeyId` is set, so EBS uses the account's default key (`aws/ebs`).

**Image Builder (recommended).** `image-builder-recipe.json` requests
`/dev/sda1` with `"encrypted": true`. Image Builder applies the recipe's
mappings to the build instance and the output AMI, so the snapshot is
encrypted even when the parent image's snapshot is not and EBS default
encryption is off. `amiTags` tag the output AMI. Image Builder does not tag
the AMI's snapshots, so tag them after the build:

```bash
AMI=<new AMI ID>
for s in $(aws ec2 describe-images --region {{ .Region }} --image-ids "$AMI" \
    --query 'Images[0].BlockDeviceMappings[?Ebs].Ebs.SnapshotId' --output text); do
  aws ec2 create-tags --region {{ .Region }} --resources "$s" \
    --tags Key=ManagedBy,Value=fabrica Key=FabricaModule,Value=workstation Key=Name,Value={{ .Name }}
done
```

**Direct EC2 bake (instance + component over SSM + `create-image`).**
Launch the bake instance with an encrypted root volume, then tag the image
and its snapshots when you create the AMI:

```bash
aws ec2 run-instances --region {{ .Region }} --image-id {{ .BaseImage }} \
  --metadata-options HttpTokens=required \
  --block-device-mappings 'DeviceName=/dev/sda1,Ebs={VolumeSize=50,VolumeType=gp3,DeleteOnTermination=true,Encrypted=true}' \
  ...   # instance type, subnet, security group, SSM instance profile

aws ec2 create-image --region {{ .Region }} --instance-id "$BAKE_INSTANCE" \
  --name "{{ .Name }}-$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M)" \
  --tag-specifications \
    'ResourceType=image,Tags=[{Key=ManagedBy,Value=fabrica},{Key=FabricaModule,Value=workstation},{Key=Name,Value={{ .Name }}}]' \
    'ResourceType=snapshot,Tags=[{Key=ManagedBy,Value=fabrica},{Key=FabricaModule,Value=workstation},{Key=Name,Value={{ .Name }}}]'
```

## Gate: verify before recording the AMI

```bash
./verify-ami.sh "$AMI"
```

Exit 0 is the only pass. It fails when an EBS snapshot is not encrypted, is
missing the `ManagedBy=fabrica` tag, or the AWS CLI call fails. Record the
AMI in `workstation.amiId` only after it passes. The bake instance, its
volume, and any pipeline resources are not Fabrica resources; clean them up
yourself.

See `docs/workstation-ami.md`.
